            formed from it and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
            between these.
          %h3#TheContinuumRealWorldLine The continuum real line
          %p
            The fundamental premise of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            as a model of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            is the following.
          %p
            %strong Premise.
            %em
              The abstract
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline"} worldline
              of any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle"} particle
              is modeled by the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum"} continuum
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
          %p This comes down to the following sequence of premises.
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                There is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+ordering"} linear ordering
                of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/points"} points
                on a
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline"} worldline
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integers"} integers
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℤ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
                \.
            %li
              %p
                These intervals may each be subdivided into
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                smaller intervals, for each natural number
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline"} worldline
                in an order-preserving way by the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"} rational numbers
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℚ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
                \.
            %li
              %p
                This labeling is dense: every point on the worldline is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supremum"} supremum
                of an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+set"} inhabited
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bounded+subset"} bounded subset
                of such labels. This means that a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline"} worldline
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} is
                the
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum"} continuum
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
                \.
          %p
            The adjective “real” in “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"}> real number
            ” is a historical shadow of the old idea that real numbers are related to
            = succeed "," do
              %em observed reality
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            in this way. The experimental success of this assumption shows that it is valid at least to very good approximation.
          %p
            Speculations are common that in a fully exact theory of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/p-adic+physics"} p-adic physics
            one explores the hypothesis that the relevant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+field"} completion
            of the rational numbers as above is not the reals, but
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/p-adic+numbers"} p-adic numbers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℚ
                    %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}_p
            for some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prime+number"} prime number
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \in \mathbb{N}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QFT+on+non-commutative+spacetime"} QFT on non-commutative spacetime
            one explore the idea that at small scales the smooth
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum"} continuum
            is to be replaced by an object in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+geometry"} noncommutative geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-commutative+analytic+space"} non-commutative analytic space
            as a potential model for
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
          %p
            For the time being all this remains speculation and differential geometry based on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum"} continuum
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
            remains the context of all fundamental
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28in+theoretical+physics%29"} model building
            in physics related to observed
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phenomenology"} phenomenology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+theory"} quantum theory
            applied to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            %em
              %a{:href => "GeometricQuantization"} Geometric quantization
            how not just
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+physics"} classical physics
            but also
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+theory"} quantum theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum"} continuum
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
          %p
            This is the motivation for studying models of physics in geometry modeled on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum"} continuum
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
            %em maximally independent
            of this specific choice (this is what
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
            accomplishes for us, discussed below
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#SmoothSpacesLayerSem"} Smooth spaces – Semantic Layer
            = succeed "\u201D," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
            %a{:href => "#SmoothSpacesLayerSem"} below
            and many of the constructions, propositions and theorems in the following will continue to hold. This is notably what we do below in
            %em
              %a{:href => "#SupergeometricCoordinateSystems"} Supergeometric coordinate systems
            when we generalize the present discussion to a flavor of differential geometry that also formalizes the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermion"} fermion
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particles"} particles
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometry
          %h3#CartesianSpaces Cartesian spaces and smooth functions
          #SmoothFunctions.num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}
              is called a
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
              if,
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coinduction"} coinductively
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivative"} derivative
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mfrac
                          %mrow
                            %mi d
                            %mi f
                          %mrow
                            %mi d
                            %mi x
                        %mo :
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mo →
                        %mi ℝ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{d f}{d x} : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}
                  exists;
              %li
                %p and is itself a smooth function.
            %p
              We write
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(\mathbb{R}) \in Set
              for the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
              of all smooth functions on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              The superscript “
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {}^\infty
              ” in “
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(\mathbb{R})
              ” refers to the order of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivatives"} derivatives
              that exist for smooth functions. More generally for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \in \mathbb{N}
              one writes
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^k(\mathbb{R})
              for the set of
              = succeed "-fold" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiable+functions"} differentiable functions
              on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
          #CartesianSpaceAndHomomorphism.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              For
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
              is the set
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathbb{R}^n = \{ (x^1 , \cdots, x^{n}) | x^i \in \mathbb{R} \}
            %p
              of
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tuples"} tuples
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi k
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \leq k \leq n
              write
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi i
                      %mi k
                    %mo :
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    i^k : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}^n
            %p
              for the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi i
                      %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i^k(x) = (0, \cdots, 0,x,0,\cdots,0)
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tuple"} tuple
              whose
              = succeed "th" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              and all whose other entries are
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \in \mathbb{R}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝕡
                      %mi k
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathbb{p}^k : \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}
            %p
              for the function such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi p
                        %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo ,
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p^k(x^1, \cdots, x^n) = x^k
            %p
              A
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
              of Cartesian spaces is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mn 2
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    f : \mathbb{R}^{n_1} \to \mathbb{R}^{n_2}
                    \\,,
            %p
              hence a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : \mathbb{R}^{n_1} \to \mathbb{R}^{n_2}
              such that all
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+derivatives"} partial derivatives
              exist and are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+map"} continuous
              (…).
          .num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              Regarding
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
              as an
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+function"} linear function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{n_1} \to \mathbb{R}^{n_2}
              is in particular a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              But a homomorphism of Cartesian spaces in def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CartesianSpaceAndHomomorphism"}
              is
              %em not
              required to be a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+map"} linear map
              %em not
              regard the Cartesian spaces here as
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+spaces"} vector spaces
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : \mathbb{R}^{n_1} \to \mathbb{R}^{n_2}
              is called a
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphism"} diffeomorphism
              if there exists another smooth function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mn 2
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{n_2} \to \mathbb{R}^{n_1}
              such that the underlying functions of sets are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse"} inverse
              to each other
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi g
                    %mo =
                    %mi id
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    f \circ g = id
            %p and
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi f
                    %mo =
                    %mi id
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    g \circ f = id
                    \\,.
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              There exists a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphism"} diffeomorphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{n_1} \to \mathbb{R}^{n_2}
              precisely if
