          %h3#PropertiesOfSmoothFunctions The magic properties of smooth functions
          %p
            Below we encounter generalizations of ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            that include explicit “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimals"}> infinitesimals
            ” in the guise of
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimally+thickened+points"} infinitesimally thickened points
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superpoints"} superpoints
            (necessary for the description of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermion+fields"} fermion fields
            such as the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+field"} Dirac field
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#FieldBundles"} below
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            in just the same way as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck"} Grothendieck
            introduced them into
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
            (in the guise of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+schemes"}> formal schemes
            ”), namely in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+dual"} formally dual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rings+of+functions"} rings of functions
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nilpotent+ideals"} nilpotent ideals
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            rests on the following three basic but important properties, which say that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
            behave “more algebraically” than their definition might superficially suggest:
          #AlgebraicFactsOfDifferentialGeometry.num_prop
