          %h3#CoverageOfDifferentiallyGoodOpenCovers The coverage of differentially good open covers
          %p
            We discuss a standard structure of a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
            on the category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CartSp"} CartSp
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sketches+of+an+Elephant"} Johnstone – Sketches of an Elephant
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+site"} small
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coverage"} coverage
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topology"} Grothendieck topology
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_8 Definition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
              the standard
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+ball"} open n-ball
              is the subset
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                    %msubsup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                      %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %munderover
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mn 2
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ↪
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    D^n = \{ (x_i)_{i =1}^n \in \mathbb{R}^n | \sum_{i = 1}^n (x_i)^2 \lt 1 \}  \hookrightarrow \mathbb{R}^n
                    \\,.
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              There is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphism"} diffeomorphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ≃
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mi n
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathbb{R}^n \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} D^n
                    \\,.
          #DifferentiallyGoodOpenCover.num_defn
            %h6#definition_9 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong
                differentially
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/good+open+cover"} good open cover
              of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
              is a set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                    %mo ↪
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{U_i \hookrightarrow \mathbb{R}^n\}
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subset"} open subset
              inclusions of Cartesian spaces such that these
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+cover"} cover
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
              and such for each non-empty finite
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersection"} intersection
              there exists a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphism"} diffeomorphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi i
                          %mn 1
                    %mo ∩
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ∩
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi i
                          %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathbb{R}^n \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} U_{i_1} \cap \cdots \cap U_{i_k}
            %p
              that identifies the
              = succeed "-fold" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_6 Remark
            %p
              Differentiably good covers are useful for computations. Their full impact is however on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+presheaves"} simplicial presheaves
              over
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CartSp"} CartSp
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+smooth+homotopy+types"} smooth homotopy types
          #DiffGoodOpenCoversRefineOpenCovers.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_4 Proposition
            %p
              Every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+cover"} open cover
              refines to a differentially good open cover, def.
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DifferentiallyGoodOpenCover"}
          %p
            A proof is at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/good+open+cover"} good open cover
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_7 Remark
            %p
              Despite its appearance, this is not quite a classical statement. The classical statement is only that every open cover is refined by a
              %em topologically
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/good+open+cover"} good open cover
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/ball#References"} here in the references-section
              at
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+ball"} open ball
              for the situation concerning this statement in the literature.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_8 Remark
            %p
              The
              %em good
              open covers do not yet form a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topology"} Grothendieck topology
              on
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CartSp"} CartSp
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covering"} covering
              family also its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
              along any morphism in the category is a covering family. But while the pullback of every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+cover"} open cover
              is again an open cover, and hence open covers form a Grothendieck topology on
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CartSp"} CartSp
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/good+open+cover"} good
              open cover is again
              = succeed "." do
                %em good
          #ExampleThatGoodOpenCoversAreNotPullbackStable.num_example
            %h6#example_5 Example
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                    %mover
                      %mo ↪
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mi i
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\mathbb{R}^2\stackrel{\phi_{i}}{\hookrightarrow}\mathbb{R}^2\}_{i \in \{1,2\}}
              be the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+cover"} open cover
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/plane"} plane
              by an open left half space
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mover
                      %mo ↪
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathbb{R}^2 \simeq \{ (x_1,x_2) \in \mathbb{R}^2 | x_1 \lt 1 \} \stackrel{\phi_1}{\hookrightarrow} \mathbb{R}^2
            %p and a right open half space
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mover
                      %mo ↪
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mn 2
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathbb{R}^2 \simeq \{ (x_1,x_2) \in \mathbb{R}^2 | x_1 \gt -1 \} \stackrel{\phi_2}{\hookrightarrow} \mathbb{R}^2
                    \\,.
            %p The intersection of the two is the open strip
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo &lt;
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ↪
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathbb{R}^2 \simeq \{ (x_1, x_2) \in \mathbb{R}^2 | -1 \lt x_1 \lt 1 \}
                    \\hookrightarrow
                    \\mathbb{R}^2
                    \\,.
            %p
              So this is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/good+open+cover"} good open cover
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^2
            %p But consider then the smooth function
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi cos
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mi π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi sin
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mi π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    2(\cos(2 \pi (-)), \sin(2 \pi (-))) \colon \mathbb{R}^1 \to \mathbb{R}^2
            %p
              which sends the line to a curve in the plane that periodically goes around the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle"} circle
              of radius 2 in the plane.
            %p
              Then the pullback of the above good open cover on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^2
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^1
              along this function is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+cover"} open cover
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
              by two open subsets, each being a disjoint union of countably many open
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intervals"} intervals
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^1
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+union"} disjoint union
              is not
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contractible+topological+space"} contractible
              (it does not contract to the point, but to many points!).
            %p
              So the pullback of the good open cover that we started with is an open cover which is not
              %em good
              anymore. But it has an evident
              %em refinement
              by a good open cover.
          %p This is a special case of what the following statement says in generality.
          #TheDifferentiallyGoodOpenCoverCoverage.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_5 Proposition
            %p
              The differentially good open covers, def.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DifferentiallyGoodOpenCover"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coverage"} coverage
              on
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CartSp"} CartSp
            %p
              Hence
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CartSp"} CartSp
              equipped with that coverage is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              By definition of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coverage"} coverage
              we need to check that for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                    %mo ↪
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{U_i \hookrightarrow \mathbb{R}^n\}_{i \in I}
              any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/good+open+cover"} good open cover
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi k
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon \mathbb{R}^k \to \mathbb{R}^n
              any smooth function, we can find a good open cover
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi K
                      %mi j
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi k
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mrow
                        %mi j
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{K_j \to \mathbb{R}^k\}_{j \in J}
              and a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                    %mo →
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J \to I
              such that for each
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j \in J
              there is a smooth function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi K
                      %mi j
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mrow
                        %mi ρ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi j
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \colon K_j \to U_{\rho(j)}
              that makes this
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagram"} diagram
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commuting+diagram"} commute
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi K
                              %mi j
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi ϕ
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi U
                              %mrow
                                %mi i
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi j
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi ℝ
                              %mi k
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi f
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi ℝ
                              %mi n
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    
                    K_j &amp;\stackrel{\phi}{\to}&amp; U_{i(j)}
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                    \\\
                    \\mathbb{R}^k &amp;\stackrel{f}{\to}&amp; \mathbb{R}^n
                    }
                    \\,.
            %p
              To obtain this, let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{f^* U_i \to \mathbb{R}^k\}
              be the pullback of the original covering family, in that
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                    %mo ≔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ↪
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi k
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    f^* U_i \coloneqq \{ x \in \mathbb{R}^k | f(x) \in U_i \}
                    \\hookrightarrow \mathbb{R}^k
                    \\,.
            %p
              This is evidently an
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+cover"} open cover
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/good+open+cover"} good open cover
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DiffGoodOpenCoversRefineOpenCovers"}
              there does exist a good open cover
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi K
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                      %mover
                        %mi j
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mo ↪
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi k
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mi j
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                        %mo ∈
                        %mover
                          %mi J
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\tilde K_{\tilde j} \hookrightarrow \mathbb{R}^k\}_{\tilde j \in \tilde J}
              %em refining
              it, which in turn means that for all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi j
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde j
              there is
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mover
                                %mi K
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                              %mover
                                %mi j
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi K
                              %mrow
                                %mi j
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mover
                                  %mi j
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi ℝ
                              %mi k
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mo =
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi ℝ
                              %mi k
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \\tilde K_{\tilde j} &amp;\to&amp; K_{j(\tilde j)}
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                    \\\
                    \\mathbb{R}^k &amp;\stackrel{=}{\to}&amp; \mathbb{R}^k
                    }
                    \\,.
            %p
              Therefore then the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pasting"} pasting
              composite of these commuting squares
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mover
                                %mi K
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                              %mover
                                %mi j
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi K
                              %mrow
                                %mi j
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mover
                                  %mi j
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi U
                              %mrow
                                %mi i
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi j
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mover
                                  %mi j
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi ℝ
                              %mi k
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mo =
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi ℝ
                              %mi k
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi f
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi ℝ
                              %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \\tilde K_{\tilde j} &amp;\to&amp; K_{j(\tilde j)} &amp;\to&amp; U_{i(j(\tilde j))}
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                    \\\
                    \\mathbb{R}^k &amp;\stackrel{=}{\to}&amp; \mathbb{R}^k
                    \&amp;\stackrel{f}{\to}&amp; \mathbb{R}^n
                    }
            %p
              solves the condition required in the definition of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coverage"} coverage
          %p
            By example
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ExampleThatGoodOpenCoversAreNotPullbackStable"}
            this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/good+open+cover"} good open cover
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coverage"} coverage
            is not a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topology"} Grothendieck topology
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaves"} sheaves
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_6 Proposition
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topology"} Grothendieck topology
              induced on
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CartSp"} CartSp
              by the differentially good open cover coverage of def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TheDifferentiallyGoodOpenCoverCoverage"}
              has as covering families the ordinary
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+covers"} open covers
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_9 Remark
            %p
              This means that for every sheaf-theoretic construction to follow we can just as well consider the Grothendieck topology of open covers on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi CartSp
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CartSp
              %em good
              open cover coverage that makes manifest, below, that sheaves on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi CartSp
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CartSp
              form a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+connected+topos"} locally connected topos
              and in consequence then a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+topos"} cohesive topos
              %a{:href => "#SmoothnGroupoids"} further below
              to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-sheaves"} (∞,1)-sheaves
              on
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CartSp"} CartSp
              %em
                %a{:href => "#InfinityConnectednessOfSmoothInfinityGrpd"} Smooth n-groupoids – Semantic Layer – Local Infinity-Connectedness
                below.
          %h3#SliceCategories The slice category
          %p (…)
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+category"} slice category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi CartSp
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi ℝ
                            %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CartSp_{\mathbb{R}^n}
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subterminal+object"}> subterminal object
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+cover"} open cover
          %p (…)
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %h2#CoordinateSystemsLayerSyn Syntactic Layer
          %p
            In this
            %a{:href => "#LayerSyn"} Syn Layer
            we discuss the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abstract+generals"} abstract generals
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                abstract
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinate+systems"} coordinate systems
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CartesianSpacesAndSmoothFunctions"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+language"} internal language
            of a category with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/products"} products
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+types"} product types
          %blockquote
            %p This is rough, needs further development.
          %h3#Judgments Judgments about types and terms
          %p
            We now introduce a different
            %em notation
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            (such as the category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CartSp"} CartSp
            of def.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CartSpCategory"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deductive+reasoning"} reason deductively
            about
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
          %p
            But before we begin to make any actual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deductions"} deductions
            about
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            in a category
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#NaturalDeductionRulesForProductTypes"} below
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgments"} judgments
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions"} propositions
            that we eventually formalize
            %em within
            this
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metalanguage"} metalanguage
          %p
            To express that there is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  X \in \mathcal{C}
          %p
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequent"} sequent
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Type
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\vdash \; X \colon Type
                  \\,.
          %p
            We say that these symbols express the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgment"} judgment
            that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Type
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vdash \; X \colon Type
            is the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntax"} syntax
            of which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \in \mathcal{C}
            is the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
          %p
            For instance the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} * \in \mathcal{C}
            we call the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            of the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+type"} unit type
            and write
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntax"} syntactically
            as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi Unit
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Type
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\vdash \; Unit \colon Type
                  \\,.
          %p
            If we want to express that we do assume that a terminal object indeed exists, hence that we want to be able to
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deduction"} deduce
            the existence of a terminal object from no hypothesis, then we write this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgment"} judgment
            below a horizontal line
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi Unit
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi Type
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{}{\vdash \; Unit \colon Type}
                  \\,.
          %p
            We will see more interesting such horizontal-line statements
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#NaturalDeductionRulesForProductTypes"} below
          %p
            Next, to express an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/element"} element
            of the object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi x
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  * \stackrel{x}{\to} X
          %p
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            we write equivalently the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequent"} sequent
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\vdash \; x \colon X
          %p
            and call this the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgment"} judgment
            that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            is a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term"} term
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Notice that every object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \in \mathcal{C}
            becomes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
            in the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+category"} slice category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}_{/X}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to X
            be any morphism in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+category"} slice category
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi X
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  A \in \mathcal{C}_{/X}
                  \\,.
          %p
            We declare that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntax"} syntax
            of which this is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            is given by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequent"} sequent
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi X
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Type
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  x \colon X \;\vdash \;  A(x) \colon Type
                  \\,.
          %p
            We say that this expresses the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgement"} judgement
            that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is an
            = succeed ";" do
              %em
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                \-
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type"} dependent type
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
              of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+variable"} free variable
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Notice that an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/element"} element
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \in \mathcal{C}_{/X}
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+element"} generalized element
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to A
            which fits into a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commuting+diagram"} commuting diagram
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi a
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mrow
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi id
                                  %mi X
                          %mo ↘
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo ↙
                            %mrow
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  X &amp;&amp;\stackrel{a}{\to}&amp;&amp; A
                  \\\
                  \&amp; {}_{\mathllap{id_X}}\searrow &amp;&amp; \swarrow_{}
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; X
                  }
          %p
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
          %p
            We declare that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntax"} syntax
            of which such
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi in
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  a \in A  \;\;\;\; (in \mathcal{C}_{/X})
          %p
            is this the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequent"} sequent
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi X
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  x\colon X \;\vdash\; a(x) : A(x)
                  \\,.
          %p
            We say that this expresses the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgment"} judgment
            that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a(x)
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term+in+context"} term depending on
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+variable"} free variable
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            This completes the list of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgment"} judgment
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntax"} syntax
            to be considered. Notice that if the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            has
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/products"} products
            then, even though it does not explicitly appear above, this is sufficient to express any morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi f
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \stackrel{f}{\to} Y
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semantics"} semantics
            of a
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term"} term
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+category"} slice category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}_{/X}
            which as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commuting+diagram"} commuting diagram
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            itself is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi f
                              %mo ,
                              %msub
                                %mi id
                                %mi X
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi Y
                          %mo ×
                          %mi X
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mrow
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi id
                                  %mi X
                          %mo ↘
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo ↙
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi p
                                  %mn 2
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  X &amp;&amp; \stackrel{(f,id_X)}{\to} &amp;&amp;  Y\times X
                  \\\
                  \&amp; {}_{\mathllap{id_X}}\searrow &amp;&amp; \swarrow_{\mathrlap{p_2}}
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; X
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            This is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            for which the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntax"} syntax
            is simply
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi X
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Y
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  x \colon X \;\vdash\; y(x) \colon Y
                  \\,,
          %p
            being the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgment"} judgment
            which expresses that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y(x)
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term+in+context"} term in context
            of an
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type"} dependent type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            in the special degenerate case that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            does not actually vary with
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \colon X
          %h3#NaturalDeductionRulesForProductTypes Natural deduction rules for product types
          %p
            With the
            %a{:href => "#Judgments"} above symbolic notation
            for making
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgments"} judgments
            about the presence of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deduction"} deduction
            that tells us how we may
            %em process
            these symbols (how to do
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computations"} computations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            that we can build out of the given ones by
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+constructions"} universal constructions
            in the sense of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
          %p
            This way of
            %em deducing
            new expressions from given ones is very basic as well as very natural and hence goes by the technical term
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+deduction"} natural deduction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            (every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+construction"} universal construction
            in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+deduction"} natural deduction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deductive+reasoning"} deductively reason
            about it. This set of rules, in turn, always consists of four kinds of rules, called the
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+formation+rule"} type formation rule
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term+introduction+rule"} term introduction rule
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term+elimination+rule"} term elimination rule
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computation+rule"}> computation rule
                \.
          %p
            These are going to be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntax"} syntax
            in
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            of which
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+constructions"} universal constructions
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            is the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
          %p
            In our running example where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo =
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C} =
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CartSp"} CartSp
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+construction"} universal construction
            available is that of forming
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/products"} products
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+deduction"} natural deduction
            rules by way of example of the special case of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+types"} product types
          %p
            %strong
              1.
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+formation+rule"} type formation rule
            Let
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  A , B \in \mathcal{C}
          %p
            be two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/products"} products
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+the"} the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi B
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  A \times B \in \mathcal{C}
                  \\,.
          %p
            We now declare that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntax"} syntax
            of which this state of affairs is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            is the collection of symbols of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi Type
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi Type
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ×
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi Type
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{A \colon Type \;\;\;\;\; B \colon Type}{ A \times B \colon Type}
                  \\,.
          %p
            Here on top of the horizontal line we have the two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgments"} judgments
            which express that,
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntax"} syntactically
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semantics"} semantically
            that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \in \mathcal{C}
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \in \mathcal{C}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgment"} judgment
            which expresses that there is, syntactically, a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+type"} product type
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mi B
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \times B \in \mathcal{C}
          %p
            %strong Remark (Computation)
            All this may seem, on first sight, like being a lot of fuss about something of grandiose banality. To see what is gradually being accomplished here despite of this appearance, as we proceed in this discussion, the reader can and should think of this as the first steps in the definition of a
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/programming+language"} programming language
            = succeed "-step" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+deduction"} natural deduction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+formation+rule"} type formation rule
            above is an operation that this computer validates as being an allowed step of transforming a memory state with a given collection of such strings into a new memory state to which the string below the horizontal line is added. As we add the remaining rules below, what looks like a grandiose banality so far will remain grandiose, but no longer be a banality. The reader feeling in need of more motivational remarks along these lines might want to take a break here and have a look at the entry
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computational+trinitarianism"} computational trinitarianism
            first, that provides more pointers to the grandiose picture which we are approaching here.
          %p
            Next, the second
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+deduction"} natural deduction
            rule for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+types"} product types
            is the
          %p
            %strong>
              2.
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term+elimination+rule"} term elimination rule
            \. The fact that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi B
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \times B \in \mathcal{C}
            is equipped with two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projection"} projection
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                          %mover
                            %mo ←
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi p
                                %mn 1
                          %mi A
                          %mo ×
                          %mi B
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi p
                                %mn 2
                          %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  A \stackrel{p_1}{\leftarrow} A \times B \stackrel{p_2}{\to} B
                  }
          %p
            means that from every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/element"} element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \times B
            we may deduce the existence of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elements"} elements
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_1(t)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_2(t)
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            of which the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+deduction"} natural deduction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntax"} syntax
            is:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ×
                      %mi B
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi A
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ×
                      %mi B
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi B
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{\vdash \; t \colon A \times B}{\vdash \; p_1(t) \colon A}
                  \\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;
                  \\frac{\vdash \; t \colon A \times B}{\vdash \; p_2(t) \colon B}
                  \\,.
          %p
            As before, this is to say that if syntactically we are given strings of symbols expressing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgments"} judgments
            as on the top of these horizontal lines, then we may “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+deduction"}> naturally deduce
            ” also the judgment of the string of symbols on the bottom of this line.
          %p
            %strong>
              3.
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term+introduction+rule"} term introduction rule
            \. The first part of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+property"} universal property
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            is that for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q \in \mathcal{C}
            any other
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            equipped with morphisms
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi Q
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mrow
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mi a
                          %mo ↙
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo ↘
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mi b
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; Q
                  \\\
                  \&amp; {}^{\mathllap{a}}\swarrow &amp;&amp; \searrow^{\mathrlap{b}}
                  \\\
                  A  &amp;&amp; &amp;&amp;  B
                  }
          %p
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Q \to A \times B
          %p
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            of which the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+deduction"} natural deduction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntax"} syntax
            is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi B
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ×
                      %mi B
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{
                  \\vdash\; a \colon A \;\;\;\;\;\; \vdash\; b \colon B
                  }{
                  \\vdash (a,b) \colon A \times B
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            With the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elements"} elements
            that are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semantics"} semantics
            of the terms appearing here made explicit, this is the syntax for a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagram"} diagram
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi Q
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mrow
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mi a
                          %mo ↙
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi a
                                %mo ,
                                %mi b
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo ↘
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mi b
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                          %mo ×
                          %mi B
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi B
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; Q
                  \\\
                  \&amp; {}^{\mathllap{a}}\swarrow &amp;\downarrow^{\mathrlap{(a,b)}}&amp; \searrow^{\mathrlap{b}}
                  \\\
                  A &amp;&amp; A \times B &amp;&amp; B
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            %strong>
              4.
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computation+rule"} computation rule
            \. The next part of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+property"} universal property
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            is that the resulting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagram"} diagram
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi Q
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mrow
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mi a
                          %mo ↙
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo ↘
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mi b
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ←
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi p
                                %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %mi A
                          %mo ×
                          %mi B
                        %mtd
